# espix — ESP32-S31 with in-package PSRAM (WROOM-3: 16 MB flash, 8/16 MB PSRAM).
#
# The PSRAM is always octal and always 250 MHz (there is no mode or speed to
# choose), and IDF detects its size at runtime, so there is no compile-time
# difference between the PSRAM variants -- which is why the board identity is
# plain `s31`, with no PSRAM suffix. The image is built for the WROOM-3's 16 MB
# flash; the loader provisions the partition table for whatever flash it finds,
# from the 8, 16 and 32 MB tables it carries.

CONFIG_IDF_TARGET="esp32s31"

#
# Flash
#
# The WROOM-3 is a 16 MB part, and IDF puts the flash size in the image header and
# refuses to probe a chip larger than it. The image is still flashed with the
# smallest table (8 MB), which is valid on any larger chip; the loader writes the
# table that matches the physical flash. See docs/OTA.md 11.
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_16MB=y
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM_FILENAME="partitions/esp32s31-8mb.csv"
# DIO, IDF's own default for this target (the S3 uses QIO).
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHMODE_DIO=y


#
# PSRAM — apps are loaded here, so it is not optional on this target.
#
CONFIG_SPIRAM_SPEED_250M=y

# XIP-from-PSRAM is inherited from the target-independent defaults (it is what
# closes the flash-cache crash documented there).
# Load relocated ELF text/data into PSRAM (this is the elf_loader, not XIP).
# There is no bus-address mirror to rely on as on the S3; the component
# handles the target itself.
#
# CONFIG_ELF_LOADER_LOAD_PSRAM is in sdkconfig.defaults.

#
# CPU
#
CONFIG_ESP_DEFAULT_CPU_FREQ_MHZ_320=y

#
# Networking: the S31 has a Gigabit Ethernet MAC. The option lives in
# components/espix_net/Kconfig and is on by default wherever SOC_EMAC_SUPPORTED.
#
CONFIG_ESPIX_ETH_ENABLED=y

#
# Bluetooth -- the S31 has Classic as well as BLE, which the S3 does not, so
# A2DP/HFP live here. Bluedroid is the host that supports Classic.
#
CONFIG_ESPIX_BT=y

# The board's ES8311: a speaker that needs no Bluetooth. Pins are the S31
# function coreboard's, from the board manager's config.
CONFIG_ESPIX_I2S=y

# NB: CONFIG_ESP_SYSTEM_USE_FRAME_POINTER would make watchdog dumps readable,
# but it adds ~152 KB of code -- enough to overflow the 3.5 MB kernel slot. Not
# on this part; the coredump backtrace is the tool for real faults.

# Audio playback (play). The decoder set -- MP3 and the WAV container, and
# nothing else -- lives in the common sdkconfig.defaults, because it describes
# what espix ships rather than what the S31 is.
#
# Resampling is still OFF, and not because the part cannot do it: the S31 has a
# hardware ASRC (CONFIG_SOC_ASRC_SUPPORTED; esp_asrc, no GMF needed). The engine
# has no resampler yet, so a source must match the sink's negotiated rate. SBC
# sinks pick 44.1 kHz in practice (the soundcore Q45 does), which a CD-rate MP3
# already is; the hardware ASRC is the phase-2 fix for everything else.
CONFIG_BT_ENABLED=y
CONFIG_BT_BLUEDROID_ENABLED=y
CONFIG_BT_CLASSIC_ENABLED=y
CONFIG_BT_A2DP_ENABLE=y
CONFIG_BT_BLE_ENABLED=n
# Bluedroid's own allocations from PSRAM first, where it applies: measured on
# this board it is only ~5 kB, but it is free and it is what the esp_bt_audio
# example does.
CONFIG_BT_ALLOCATION_FROM_SPIRAM_FIRST=y
# Cover art is the AVRCP controller fetching artwork from the sink. We consume
# none, and it is not on the playback path, so it is off.
# CONFIG_BT_AVRCP_CT_COVER_ART_ENABLED is not set
# Classic only: the controller must be in BR/EDR mode for A2DP (IDF's own
# a2dp_source example sets these three for exactly this reason).
CONFIG_BTDM_CTRL_MODE_BLE_ONLY=n
CONFIG_BTDM_CTRL_MODE_BR_EDR_ONLY=y
CONFIG_BTDM_CTRL_MODE_BTDM=n


# littlefs I/O units were tried here (read_size 128->512, cache_size 512->4096,
# lookahead 128->1024) and measured: about 10% faster (read per second of audio
# ~1150-1330 ms down to ~1076-1245) for 4 kB of internal RAM gone (30 kB free
# -> 13 kB). Not worth it while internal is the scarce pool, and not the real
# limit either -- the USB path is just as slow, at the same ~180 kB/s.
# See docs/ROADMAP.md.

#
# Console
#
# The primary console is UART0 on the external chip, which is what this board is
# wired for. IDF also mirrors output to the USB-Serial/JTAG secondary by
# default, and on a board with a real UART that is redundant and not free:
# every log line is written twice, and the USB-Serial/JTAG write spins when the
# host is not draining it -- exactly the case while OpenOCD holds the port.
# Off, so the USB-DBG port carries JTAG and nothing else.
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_SECONDARY_NONE=y

#
# Audio codec
#
# Two library variants exist for S31 -- lib/esp32s31 (assembly) and
# lib/esp32s31_esp_c (portable C) -- selected by ESP_AUDIO_CODEC_S31_USE_ASM,
# which 2.6.2 defaults to n. Keep the default. Disassembling both shows the
# assembly variant uses the PIE coprocessor only in LC3 (114 sites) and Opus
# (19), and not in MP3 at all; and on S31 only core 1 has PIE, so FreeRTOS pins
# the calling task to core 1 on its first PIE instruction (IDF's own
# freertos/test_apps/freertos/port/test_pie_in_task.c says so). No MP3 gain,
# and a core-affinity constraint not worth taking on yet. Revisit for LE Audio.
# CONFIG_ESP_AUDIO_CODEC_S31_USE_ASM is not set

# A TCP send buffer that can hold a frame.
#
# lwIP defaults to 5760 bytes, which is smaller than one incremental VNC frame,
# so handing one over takes several round trips however fast the link is. An
# in-bounds drag was measured on the same path at 32K and at 114688: updates of
# 16-64 KB went from 7.7 to 11.4 MB/s on the wire and updates over 64 KB from
# 6.5 to 10.1 MB/s, so the worst update still cost about the same ~16 ms even
# though the largest grew from 101 KB to 155 KB. Updates under 4 KB are
# unchanged -- that time is per-write overhead, not the buffer.
#
# setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF) cannot do it: lwIP defines it and does not implement it,
# and TCP_SNDBUF is not a socket option either. TCP_SND_BUF is the only lever.
#
# It is a ceiling on *queued* bytes, not a reservation, so it does not make every
# connection take this much -- bytes are allocated only as they are queued. And
# they are not internal RAM: lwIP here is MEM_LIBC_MALLOC + MEMP_MEM_MALLOC (heap,
# not static pools), and CONFIG_SPIRAM_TRY_ALLOCATE_WIFI_LWIP makes that heap
# PSRAM-first. So a busy VNC queue costs PSRAM, not the internal RAM Bluetooth
# needs.
#
# 114688 needs window scaling to exceed the 65535 the range otherwise allows, but
# TCP_RCV_SCALE=0 advertises an unscaled receive window, so only the send side
# grows. Nothing else moves with it: TCP_SND_QUEUELEN is derived from TCP_SND_BUF
# in opt.h, and the MEMP_NUM_* / PBUF_POOL_SIZE counts are heap-backed here.
#
# It is 114688 rather than more because lwIP derives TCP_SNDLOWAT = TCP_SND_BUF/2
# and its sanity check (init.c) requires that to stay 4*MSS below 0xFFFF, which
# caps TCP_SND_BUF near 119 KB unless TCP_SNDLOWAT is overridden too -- and IDF
# exposes no Kconfig for that. A 155 KB update still spills past it, but the
# ceiling is what keeps the per-byte wire cost down; going higher would mean
# overriding the low-water mark as well.
#
# The receive window stays at 5760. Raising it broke the OTA: a large SFTP upload
# stalled partway -- "peer stopped N bytes into a 2064-byte packet" -- while
# everything else was fine, because the upload is the one path that depends on how
# much a peer may have in flight. Against LWIP_TCP_RECVMBOX_SIZE=6 and a 1440-byte
# MSS that is marginal, and an overflowing receive mailbox drops packets rather
# than back-pressuring.


# The task watchdog keeps its idle-task check, at the slowest interval IDF allows
# (the Kconfig range is 1..60). On this target it is the Timer Group hardware
# MWDT, not a software timer -- a down-counter with no periodic interrupt -- so a
# long period costs nothing while it is not firing. What it still catches is the
# one thing nothing else does: a task that stopped making progress, including one
# wedged holding a lock, which is not a fault and so never reaches the panic path.
# PANIC stays off, so this reports and never resets.

